// The Interceptor interface: how a control plane plugs into AIntercept.
//
// The customer runs a gRPC server implementing Interceptor; AIntercept's
// observer is the client and calls it for every intercepted call. One
// integration serves both modes, with the same callbacks and the same
// messages:
//
//   observe  Callbacks are delivered asynchronously. Traffic never waits, and
//            a slow or unavailable Interceptor never affects it. Verdicts are
//            recorded as what would have happened (dry-run), never applied.
//   enforce  OnCallStart and OnMessage are in the request path: Envoy waits
//            for the verdict and AIntercept applies it. If the Interceptor
//            doesn't answer within the deadline, or fails, the call is
//            refused (fail closed; configurable).
//
// Which traffic is enforced is deployment configuration (hosts and paths),
// not something the Interceptor decides. Call.mode tells the Interceptor
// whether its verdict will be applied.
//
// Content: OnMessage carries request and response bodies, and is only called
// when the deployment enables Interceptor content (a setting independent of
// what AIntercept records). Without it, the Interceptor sees per-call
// metadata only: OnCallStart and OnCallComplete.
//
// See docs/INTERCEPTOR.md for wiring, deadlines and failure behaviour.

